    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Gandhi worked out his method of nonviolent resistance, satyagraha, during 21 years in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a> and brought it to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> in 1915.</li><li>As leader of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-national-congress">Indian National Congress</a> from 1921 he turned the demand for self-rule into mass campaigns, among them the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salt-march">Salt March</a> of 1930 and the Quit <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> movement of 1942.</li><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> became independent in August 1947 through a partition he opposed, and he was assassinated by a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> nationalist on 30 January 1948.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>As a law student in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> Gandhi was too shy to read out his own defence of a fellow member at a committee meeting of the London Vegetarian Society, so the president had another member read it for him.</p></aside>
    </section>

